<article class="env-corollary" id="01C9">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01C9">Corollary <span data-tag="01C9">11.9.7.4</span>.</a> Let $X$ be the inverse limit of a tower of simplicial sets </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \cdots \xrightarrow {q_3} X(3) \xrightarrow {q_2} X(2) \xrightarrow {q_1} X(1) \xrightarrow {q_0} X(0). \]
</div>
<p> Assume that each of the simplicial sets $X(n)$ is weakly contractible and that each of the morphisms $q_ n$ is a Kan fibration. Then the inverse limit $X = \varprojlim _{n \geq 0} X(n)$ is weakly contractible. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      If each of these simplicial sets $X(n)$ is weakly contractible, then each of the morphisms $q_ n: X(n+1) \rightarrow X(n)$ is a weak homotopy equivalence. Applying Proposition <a data-tag="00ZP" href="/tag/00ZP">3.3.7.6</a>, we conclude that each $q_ n$ is a trivial Kan fibration. Variant <a data-tag="01C8" href="/tag/01C8">11.9.7.3</a> then guarantees that the projection map $X \rightarrow X(0)$ is also a trivial Kan fibration. Since $X(0)$ is weakly contractible, it follows that $X$ is also weakly contractible. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
